--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULT — measured taper vs the shipped power law
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   drive  knob |      (A) R      (B) R   agree |  MODEL (1-x)^2.5 |          stage gain |  level err
    9:30  0.25 |     65.13k     65.41k    0.4% |           48.71k | meas 15.18 mdl 17.18 |     -1.99 dB
    noon  0.50 |     25.36k     25.95k    2.3% |           17.68k | meas 21.59 mdl 24.09 |     -2.50 dB
    2:30  0.75 |      6.27k      6.38k    1.6% |            3.12k | meas 30.12 mdl 33.15 |     -3.03 dB
  'agree' = |A-B|/mean. A uses phase + a delay model; B never looks at a phase. They
  fail differently, so agreement is real evidence, not a shared assumption.
  'level err' = measured stage gain MINUS model stage gain at that knob, bleed removed.
  POSITIVE = the real pedal drives the CD4049 HARDER there than the model does.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SENSITIVITY — the x=1 anchor assumes ZERO pot end resistance (the only non-fact used)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    end R |         9:30         noon         2:30
       0R |       65.41k       25.95k        6.38k
     500R |       68.01k       28.48k        7.53k
    1000R |       70.28k       30.87k        8.67k
  A real 100k pot's end resistance is typically <1% (<1k). If the ordering/conclusion
  is stable across this range, the zero-end-R anchor is not load-bearing.

============================================================================================
READ-OUT
============================================================================================
  * The MEASURED interior resistances above are what a proper C-taper curve must hit in
    step (2). Do NOT fit another power-law exponent to them — the shape FAMILY is the
    thing under suspicion, and a power law is pinned by its two endpoints (which are
    already anchored here), so it has exactly ONE degree of freedom to hit THREE points.
  * 'gain err' is the direct answer to session 15's blocker: it is the dB by which the
    real pedal drives the CD4049 harder (or softer) than the model at each knob, with
    the bleed removed. A positive noon entry is the missing noon level.
